analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment in the constructor:

```java
public ArrayList(int initialCapacity) {
    if (initialCapacity > 0) {
        this.elementData = new Object[initialCapacity];
    } else if (initialCapacity == 0) {
        this.elementData = EMPTY_ELEMENTDATA;
    } else {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Illegal Capacity: "+
                                           initialCapacity);
    }
    // @@@ If the initial capacity is valid (non-negative), the newly constructed list is empty.
}
``We need to express: if initial capacity is valid (non-negative), then after construction the list is empty. Means size == 0 and isEmpty() true, maybe also getCount? In this class, there is size field and size() method. The observer methods available: contains, get, indexOf, isEmpty, size, toArray, etc. Use this.isEmpty() maybe. So assert: (initialCapacity >= 0) => this.isEmpty() && this.size() == 0.

Also need to ensure that after throwing exception case, the assertion not required because precondition fails. So we use implication.

Thus final assert: assert (initialCapacity >= 0) => (this.isEmpty() && this.size() == 0);

But need to use \old? Not needed because we are in constructor, old values not relevant.

Thus output:

```java
assert (initialCapacity >= 0) => (this.isEmpty() && this.size() == 0);
```

Check syntax: Use parentheses. Should we use && inside implication: (A => B && C) is ambiguous because => lower priority, so need parentheses: (initialCapacity >= 0) => (this.isEmpty() && this.size() == 0). Good.

Return final.assistantfinal<code>assert (initialCapacity >= 0) => (this.isEmpty() && this.size() == 0);</code>
